1. Salesforce — Apex Triggers, Order of Execution & Bulkification | 55 min

Suppose Opportunity update hui and uske basis par related Account bhi update karna hai.

Basic trigger:

trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity (after update) {

    for(Opportunity opp : Trigger.new) {

        if(opp.StageName == 'Closed Won') {

            Account acc = new Account(
                Id = opp.AccountId,
                Customer_Status__c = 'Active'
            );

            update acc;
        }
    }
}

Functionally ye chal sakta hai, but interview me is code ko red flag samjho.

Problem:

update acc;

loop ke andar hai.

Salesforce transactions par Governor Limits lagata hai. Agar 200 Opportunities ek saath process hui, code potentially 200 DML operations attempt karega.

Instead:

trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity (after update) {

    List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();

    for(Opportunity opp : Trigger.new) {

        if(opp.StageName == 'Closed Won' && opp.AccountId != null) {

            accountsToUpdate.add(
                new Account(
                    Id = opp.AccountId,
                    Customer_Status__c = 'Active'
                )
            );
        }
    }

    if(!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
        update accountsToUpdate;
    }
}

Now regardless of whether 1 or 200 Opportunities arrive, we perform one bulk DML statement.

But ek aur bug hai.

Imagine Opportunity already Closed Won thi and kisi ne sirf Description update ki.

Current condition:

opp.StageName == 'Closed Won'

still true.

Account unnecessary update hoga.

Use Trigger.oldMap:

if(
    opp.StageName == 'Closed Won' &&
    Trigger.oldMap.get(opp.Id).StageName != 'Closed Won'
) {
    // Stage actually changed to Closed Won
}

Ye interview me important distinction hai:

Current state check vs state transition check.


---

Trigger contexts

Remember this mental model:

Context	Typical use

before insert	Set/default values before save
before update	Modify same record before update
before delete	Validate/prevent deletion
after insert	Work requiring record Id
after update	Related-record processing
after delete	Related cleanup
after undelete	Restore related logic


Key rule:

In a before trigger, same record modify karne ke liye DML nahi chahiye.

trigger AccountTrigger on Account (before insert) {

    for(Account acc : Trigger.new) {
        acc.Description = 'New Customer';
    }
}

Don't do:

update Trigger.new;

That is unnecessary and problematic.


---

Trigger.new vs Trigger.old

For update:

Trigger.new
↓
new/current values

Trigger.old
↓
values before update

Maps:

Trigger.newMap.get(recordId);
Trigger.oldMap.get(recordId);

Example:

Account oldAcc = Trigger.oldMap.get(acc.Id);

if(acc.Rating != oldAcc.Rating) {
    // Rating changed
}


---

SOQL bulkification

Another classic bad pattern:

for(Contact con : Trigger.new) {

    Account acc = [
        SELECT Id, Name
        FROM Account
        WHERE Id = :con.AccountId
    ];
}

Instead collect IDs:

Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();

for(Contact con : Trigger.new) {
    if(con.AccountId != null) {
        accountIds.add(con.AccountId);
    }
}

Query once:

Map<Id, Account> accountMap =
    new Map<Id, Account>([
        SELECT Id, Name
        FROM Account
        WHERE Id IN :accountIds
    ]);

Then:

for(Contact con : Trigger.new) {

    Account acc = accountMap.get(con.AccountId);

    if(acc != null) {
        // logic
    }
}

This pattern is worth memorising:

Collect → Query → Process → DML

You'll use it constantly in Apex.


---

Trigger Handler pattern

MNC interviews may ask:

“Do you write all business logic inside triggers?”

Good answer:

> No. I prefer keeping the trigger lightweight and moving business logic into handler/service classes. It improves maintainability, testing and reuse.



Example:

trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity (
    before insert,
    before update,
    after insert,
    after update
) {

    OpportunityTriggerHandler handler =
        new OpportunityTriggerHandler();

    if(Trigger.isAfter && Trigger.isUpdate) {
        handler.afterUpdate(Trigger.new, Trigger.oldMap);
    }
}

Handler:

public class OpportunityTriggerHandler {

    public void afterUpdate(
        List<Opportunity> newRecords,
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oldMap
    ) {

        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();

        for(Opportunity opp : newRecords) {

            Opportunity oldOpp = oldMap.get(opp.Id);

            if(
                opp.StageName == 'Closed Won' &&
                oldOpp.StageName != 'Closed Won' &&
                opp.AccountId != null
            ) {
                accountIds.add(opp.AccountId);
            }
        }

        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();

        for(Id accountId : accountIds) {

            accountsToUpdate.add(
                new Account(
                    Id = accountId,
                    Customer_Status__c = 'Active'
                )
            );
        }

        if(!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update accountsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}

Notice Set<Id>.

Why not List<Id>?

Because multiple Opportunities may belong to the same Account.

Set automatically removes duplicates.

That's exactly the sort of tiny design choice interviewers notice.

Salesforce interview drill

Try answering these without looking back:

1. Why shouldn't SOQL be inside a loop?


2. Why shouldn't DML be inside a loop?


3. Difference between Trigger.new and Trigger.old?


4. When would you use before update instead of after update?


5. Why use Set<Id> while collecting related IDs?


6. What is bulkification?


7. Why use a Trigger Handler?


8. What happens if 200 records enter your trigger?


9. How would you detect that Stage changed specifically from X → Y?


10. Can we modify Trigger.new directly in an after trigger?



Interview-level answer to #6:

> Bulkification means designing Apex so that it can process multiple records in a single transaction efficiently while staying within Salesforce governor limits. I avoid SOQL and DML inside loops, collect record IDs using Sets, query related data once, use Maps for efficient lookup and perform DML on collections.



That's the level of answer I want you giving recruiters.


