2. Core Java — OOP concepts | 25 min

Salesforce interviews sometimes test Java because Apex syntax/concepts are closely related.

Today: Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, Abstraction.

Consider:

class Employee {

    private String name;
    private double salary;

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}

name is private.

External code cannot directly do:

employee.name = "Rachel";

It goes through methods.

That's encapsulation.

Inheritance

class Employee {

    void work() {
        System.out.println("Working");
    }
}

class Developer extends Employee {

    void code() {
        System.out.println("Writing code");
    }
}

Developer inherits Employee behaviour.

Polymorphism

class Employee {

    void work() {
        System.out.println("Employee working");
    }
}

class Developer extends Employee {

    @Override
    void work() {
        System.out.println("Developer coding");
    }
}

Then:

Employee emp = new Developer();
emp.work();

Output:

Developer coding

Runtime chooses overridden implementation.

That's runtime polymorphism / method overriding.

Abstraction

abstract class Payment {

    abstract void processPayment();

}

Subclass:

class CreditCardPayment extends Payment {

    @Override
    void processPayment() {
        System.out.println("Processing credit card");
    }
}

Abstraction defines what must happen, while implementation decides how.

Interview trap

Overloading vs overriding

Overloading:

calculate(int a, int b)
calculate(double a, double b)

Same method name, different parameters.

Overriding:

Parent:

void work()

Child provides its own:

@Override
void work()

Remember:

Overloading → compile-time polymorphism

Overriding → runtime polymorphism


